proofs.proof_tex:
Apply \texttt{thm:one-sided-minimax-length-frontier} to the class \(\mathfrak M_T^{\mathrm{low}}(\alpha,\beta,L,c,\varepsilon)\), the prespecified deterministic follow-up sequence \(\Delta_T\), and the interval of \texttt{def:hybrid-interval}.  It gives a finite constant \(C_\eta\), depending only on the fixed class parameters and \(\eta\), such that
\[
 \inf_{(P,\Pi,T)\in\mathfrak M_T^{\mathrm{low}}}
 P\{\tau\in I_{T,\Delta}\}\geq1-\eta-o(1)                            \tag{1}
\]
and
\[
 \sup_{(P,\Pi,T)\in\mathfrak M_T^{\mathrm{low}}}
 \mathbb E_{P,\Pi}|I_{T,\Delta}|\leq C_\eta r_{T,\Delta}.             \tag{2}
\]
The infimum in (1) is over the entire one-sided class, so its remainder is uniform rather than pointwise.  By \texttt{def:one-sided-policy-class}, this class imposes \(\pi_t(1)\geq ct^{-\alpha}\) and \(\pi_t(0)\geq\varepsilon\), with no rare-arm upper envelope.  The quotas in (1)--(2) are exactly the deterministic quotas generated by these lower envelopes, and the interval is exactly \(I_{T,\Delta}\).  Thus (1) is the claimed uniform honesty and (2) is the claimed expected-length bound.
